A vacuum that fails between its rename and its commit leaves its compacted
copy under its final name, one per retry under a persistent fault. Reverts
#304. The rig sees an open that runs a recovery pass per leftover copy and
does not finish within the open timeout once the copies pile up.
The nightly caught it once (run 37095498277, 58 copies, episode 11).
Rerunning that seed for 40 minutes did not, twice under release and once
under releasedbg: the copies pile up only when a fault keeps vacuum failing
after its rename, and the open must then outlast a wall-clock timeout.
Expected: caught (rare).
Guarded-by: "vacuum that fails between its rename and its commit removes its copy"

diff --git a/bytecaskdb/db_vacuum.cpp b/bytecaskdb/db_vacuum.cpp
index f21ae69..ad32085 100644
--- a/bytecaskdb/db_vacuum.cpp
+++ b/bytecaskdb/db_vacuum.cpp
@@ -338,8 +338,7 @@ auto DB::vacuum_compact_file(std::uint32_t file_id, const VacuumOptions &opts)
   // rotation minted. Frames the hint scan admits under the buffer pool are
   // left to eviction if the copy goes: they are keyed by the copy's serial,
   // which is never reused.
-  cleanup.paths = {dir_ / (stem + ".hint.tmp"), dir_ / (stem + ".hint"),
-                   final_data_path};
+  cleanup.armed = false;
   // The source is unlinked once this commits. Were that unlink durable and
   // the rename not, the next open would find only .data.tmp, delete it as
   // staging, and lose every live entry the source held.
